From cbd234f71a57ef6e90cad71dc20c8c8d988fca79 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Niri-RDP rebase <niri-rdp@localhost>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 14:04:43 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 11/12] image-copy-capture: don't echo a client's own pointer
 motion to its cursor sessions

A remote desktop server (the Niri-RDP bridge) moves the cursor with a
zwlr_virtual_pointer_v1 created for its own wl_seat and draws the cursor on
the remote client from an ext-image-copy-capture cursor session on the same
connection. Every position it caused came back as a cursor `position`
event, which the server must forward as a pointer warp, snapping the
remote client's newer local cursor back while it moves.

Remember which client's virtual pointer (identified by the seat it was
created for) moved the cursor last in each event loop iteration and where
it left it. If the cursor is still there on the next cursor session
refresh, that client's own sessions take the position as known instead of
receiving it. Everything else is unchanged:

- enter and leave are always sent, as is the initial position of a new
  session;
- compositor warps (warp-mouse-to-focus) and motion from other pointers
  or clients are reported to every session;
- a virtual pointer created without a seat is echoed as before.

Smithay deduplicates positions against the last one it sent, so a warp
back to exactly that position after own motion is re-sent after a leave.

This replaces the motion-echo suppression of the former niri-rdp-cursor-v1
protocol (feature F13); upstream cursor sessions replace the rest of it.
